condition_name: Acute Keratitis
condition_slug: acute-keratitis
source: NSTG 2022
introduction: "Infection or inflammation of the cornea. It could be secondary to trauma, or associated with infective conjunctivitis or occur de novo."
clinical_features[1]:
  - type: Clinical Features
    features[7]: "Irritation, pain",Red eye (conjunctival congestion),"Eye discharge: watery; purulent (if bacterial)",Photophobia,"Visual impairment, depending on the site and size of ulcer and if interstitial","Hypopion, if associated with uveitis (no hypopion if viral)","Ulceration of cornea, which stains with fluoresceine; no ulcer in interstitial keratitis"
investigations[1]: "Corneal scraping for microscopy, culture and sensitivity"
treatment:
  goals[0]:
  non_drug[1]: Lateral tarsorrhaphy for exposure keratopathy
  drug[5]: "Antibiotic drops (if bacterial): Chloramphenicol eye drops 0.5%: Apply 1 drop at least every 2 hours, and then reduce the frequency as infection is controlled and continue for 48 hours after healing","Atropine drops; 1 drop, twice daily","Antivirals (if dendritic ulcer): Acyclovir; Apply 1 cm ointment 5 times daily (continue for at least 3 days after complete healing)","Antivirals: Idoxuridine 5% in dimethylsulfoxide. Adult and child over 12 years: apply to lesions 4 times daily for 4 days, starting at first sign of attack","Topical steroids: Only for interstitial keratitis where there is no active ulcer"
  adverse_reactions_and_cautions[1]: Never use topical steroids in the presence of an active microbial ulcer
  supportive_measures[0]:
differential_diagnoses[3]: Infective conjunctivitis,Acute iritis,Acute glaucoma
complications[2]: Corneal perforation,Corneal scarification
prevention[1]: Treat initial infection or trauma promptly to avoid progression to keratitis
other_investigations: null
definitive_treatment: null
prognosis: null